1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:11,950
In a good interface, every colour has a job, like players in a football team: neutral for backgrounds, a brand colour for actions, and colours with meaning.

2
00:00:12,650 --> 00:00:21,800
About one man in twelve has trouble telling some colours apart. So never use colour alone: add a word or an icon.

3
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:31,775
Turn each colour into its brightness, called luminance. Green counts most, because our eyes are most sensitive to it.

4
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:38,030
Then divide the lighter by the darker, after adding a small amount to each.

5
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:46,845
Grey seven six seven six seven six on white just passes at four point five four. Lighter greys fail.

6
00:00:48,550 --> 00:00:58,325
Contrast compares how light two colours are. Black on white is twenty-one to one. Normal text needs at least four and a half to one.

7
00:00:59,030 --> 00:01:08,530
Light grey text looks elegant in a design file, and becomes unreadable on a phone in sunlight. Now build a contrast checker.

